                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_189">Durham,
                                Durham, Benedictine cathedral priory of St Cuthbert</orgName>
                            <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval
                                Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                                Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great
                                Britain index card records the following context for the rejection:
                                'The book most likely is from Durham' (M. R. James); why he says
                                this, I cannot conjecture (R. A. B. Mynors).</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Two erased inscriptions on fols. viᵛ
                            and 1: <q>Liber ...</q>. Either might be read by ultra violet
                            lamp.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">On fol. v: <q>Pertinet ad <persName
                                    role="fmo">Joannem Wymsley</persName> ex dono <persName
                                    role="fmo">Roberti set'</persName> iuris cesarei professoris</q>
                            and a list of books <q>quos in suo bibliopolis possidebat</q> J. W. anno
                            Christi 1539.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">'<persName>Thomas
                            Darbyshier</persName>' (s. xvi) on fol. ii.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">On fol. iv 'bibliotheca elistae' (s.
                            xvi/xvii).</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given to Sidney Sussex College by
                                <persName>Thomas Mascall</persName>, formerly Fellow.</provenance>
